---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/SectionHead.astro';

const heroActions = [
	{ href: '/library/reductions', label: 'Browse the reductions', primary: true },
	{ href: '/program', label: 'See where the work stands', primary: true },
	{ href: '/', label: 'Back to the landing page' },
];

const concepts = [
	{
		tag: 'ACP',
		title: 'Adaptive Coherence Principle',
		copy:
			'A system retains future-bearing dynamics only while it occupies a nondegenerate interval between dissolution and crystallization.',
	},
	{
		tag: 'CDT',
		title: 'Crystallization Drift Theorem',
		copy:
			'Self-reinforcing mechanisms reduce conditional macrostate entropy unless adaptive coherence steering replenishes future-bearing uncertainty.',
	},
	{
		tag: 'A.20',
		title: 'Restraint-Power / coordination floor',
		copy:
			'A newer branch of the project links subsystem coordination concentration, decodable transformation, and the Heisenberg case as a quantum instantiation.',
	},
];

const reductions = [
	'Prigogine and dissipative structures',
	'Kauffman and edge-of-chaos dynamics',
	'Friston and free energy minimization',
	'Zurek and quantum Darwinism',
	'Bergstrom-Lachmann information bounds',
	'Price equation and Fisher’s fundamental theorem',
];

const implications = [
	{
		title: 'Persistence is not passive',
		copy:
			'The theory treats persistence as a sustained achievement against both entropy and over-closure, not as a default steady state.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Success creates its own danger',
		copy:
			'Mechanisms that stabilize a system can become the very source of lock-in, rigidity, and eventual loss of genuine alternatives.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Unification is structural, not metaphorical',
		copy:
			'The point is not that these literatures merely resemble each other. The claim is that they instantiate the same mathematical object under different variables.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Theory should also self-apply',
		copy:
			'The meta-theoretic branch asks whether a good unifying theory must avoid crystallizing its own inquiry space, generating more good questions than it closes.',
	},
];
---

<Layout
	title="Adaptive Coherence Principle | Theory"
	description="A first-pass theory overview of the Adaptive Coherence Principle, the Crystallization Drift Theorem, and the project’s unification claim."
>
	<PageHero
		eyebrow="Theory overview"
		title="The live conceptual map."
		asideTitle="Working draft context"
		actions={heroActions}
	>
		<p slot="copy">
			The current paper frames persistence as a structural interval problem.
			Future-bearing dynamics requires enough organization to resist dispersion and
			enough openness to resist total closure. The project then asks what drives
			systems toward each boundary, and why successful persistence so often carries
			the seed of eventual rigidity.
		</p>

		<p slot="sidebar">
			The current manuscript is the internal v0.9 working draft from April 2026.
			The site is not yet a paper mirror. It is a public explanation layer built
			around the manuscript’s present structure.
		</p>
		<div slot="sidebar" class="dense">
			<div>
				<strong>Theory core</strong>
				<p>ACP plus CDT, with coherent steering and compounding as the proof spine.</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<strong>Expansion layer</strong>
				<p>Reductions, control-theory bridges, empirical predictions, non-Gaussian bounds, and the coordination floor program.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</PageHero>

	<section class="section">
		<SectionHead
			kicker="Statements"
			title="Three claims that organize the project"
		/>

		<div class="card-grid">
			{
				concepts.map((concept) => (
					<article class="card">
						<span class="tag">{concept.tag}</span>
						<h3>{concept.title}</h3>
						<p>{concept.copy}</p>
					</article>
				))
			}
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<SectionHead
			kicker="Structure"
			title="How the argument moves"
		/>

		<div class="timeline">
			<article class="timeline-item">
				<h3>1. Fix the interval</h3>
				<p>
					Define the two absorbing boundaries: dissolution at the thermodynamic side,
					crystallization at the organizational side.
				</p>
			</article>
			<article class="timeline-item">
				<h3>2. Show persistence is maintained</h3>
				<p>
					Future-bearing systems do not survive by default. They require continuous
					work against the drift toward equilibrium.
				</p>
			</article>
			<article class="timeline-item">
				<h3>3. Prove the dual drift</h3>
				<p>
					Self-reinforcing mechanisms that hold the system together also narrow the
					space of reachable futures unless countered.
				</p>
			</article>
			<article class="timeline-item">
				<h3>4. Translate across domains</h3>
				<p>
					The theory is then reduced into multiple existing literatures to show the
					same structure appearing under different formal vocabularies.
				</p>
			</article>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<SectionHead
			kicker="Reductions"
			title="The unification claim"
			intro="The paper argues that these are not merely neighboring intuitions. They are recoverable as special cases of a single law."
		/>

		<div class="split-grid">
			<article class="callout">
				<strong>Reduction families</strong>
				<ul class="mini-list">
					{reductions.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
				</ul>
			</article>

			<article class="callout">
				<strong>Empirical side</strong>
				<p>
					The current draft states ten testable predictions with protocols and
					falsification criteria, including three predictions described as novel to the
					unification.
				</p>
			</article>

			<article class="callout">
				<strong>What remains sharpest</strong>
				<p>
					The theory is strongest when it explains why persistence and rigidity are
					co-produced, not merely adjacent. That is the project’s distinctive wager.
				</p>
			</article>

			<article class="callout">
				<strong>Newest bridge front</strong>
				<p>
					Control theory now has a standalone ACP register around viable continuation
					variety, actuator headroom, and saturation lock.
				</p>
			</article>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<SectionHead
			kicker="Implications"
			title="What this framework changes"
		/>

		<div class="card-grid">
			{
				implications.map((item) => (
					<article class="card">
						<h3>{item.title}</h3>
						<p>{item.copy}</p>
					</article>
				))
			}
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>
